<div class="version-badge" id="versionBadge">0.10.1</div>
<script>
var PROMPTS=[{"id": "P00", "seq": "00", "name": "Governance Doctrine Installer", "type": "SETUP", "class": "SETUP / GOVERNANCE", "sprintRole": "Install the agent governance contract that defines how all agents must behave in this repository", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "The repository has no agent governance contract, or the existing one is stale, incomplete, or not enforced. This is the first prompt to run on any repo that lacks agent rules.", "inspectFirst": "AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, HARNESS.md, .cursorrules, any existing agent instruction files, repo README for agent references, and branch/PR conventions.", "expectedOutput": "A tracked governance contract (AGENTS.md or equivalent) that defines: agent operating principles, instruction precedence, mandatory discipline, sprint declaration requirements, completion standard, and forbidden behaviors. Plus a validator or test that proves the contract is present.", "nextStep": "After governance is installed, run P01 to build the operational harness that the governance contract governs. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Governance file exists and is tracked, validator passes, commit SHA exists, push or PR evidence reported.", "color": "Slate", "copySheet": "P00_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "INSTALL GOVERNANCE DOCTRINE NOW. DO NOT MERELY ACKNOWLEDGE, SUMMARIZE, OR REPEAT THESE RULES.\n\nRepo: resolve the current repository from the environment.\nBranch: preserve existing work; use the current safe branch or create an isolated feature branch.\nSprint: Governance Doctrine Installation.\nLane: repository governance.\nOwned scope: the governance contract file, a validator that proves it exists, and the commit/PR that installs it.\nForbidden scope: harness infrastructure, product changes, destructive cleanup, force-push, secrets, generated junk.\nExpected artifacts: tracked governance file, enforcement validator, commit SHA, push or PR evidence.\n\nWHAT GOVERNANCE MUST DEFINE\nThe governance contract is the single source of truth for how agents operate in this repo. It must define:\n\n1. AGENT OPERATING PRINCIPLES\n   - Evidence before action\n   - Floor before furniture\n   - Bounded sprints with declared scope\n   - One writer per branch\n   - Reuse before replacing\n   - No completion without proof\n\n2. INSTRUCTION PRECEDENCE\n   When instructions conflict, the order is:\n   a. Platform, security, legal, repo-owner instructions\n   b. This governance contract\n   c. Task-specific prompts\n   d. Generic defaults\n\n3. MANDATORY SPRINT DECLARATION\n   Every writing sprint must state:\n   - repo and branch\n   - lane and mission\n   - owned scope and forbidden scope\n   - expected artifacts and validation commands\n   - proof ceiling\n\n4. COMPLETION STANDARD\n   A task is complete only when:\n   - files changed are named\n   - validation was actually run (not assumed)\n   - commit SHA exists\n   - push or PR state is reported\n   - one next command is given\n\n5. FORBIDDEN BEHAVIORS\n   - Acknowledgment without mutation\n   - Plans without execution\n   - Summaries without proof\n   - Completion claims without running checks\n   - Secret or credential exposure\n\nINSTALLATION PROCEDURE\n1. Inspect: look for existing AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, HARNESS.md, .cursorrules\n2. If a canonical governance file exists, repair it; do not create a competing one\n3. Write the governance contract as a tracked file in the repo root\n4. Add a validator (test script, manifest check, or CI rule) that fails if the governance file is missing or malformed\n5. Commit with message: docs(governance): install agent governance doctrine\n6. Push and open or update a PR\n\nVALIDATE\n- Governance file exists and is tracked by git\n- Validator passes (test or script confirms file is present and well-formed)\n- git diff --check passes\n- Commit SHA recorded\n- Push or PR evidence recorded\n\nFINAL REPORT\nRepo, branch, governance file path, validator result, commit SHA, push or PR state, and one exact next command.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["governance", "doctrine", "install doctrine", "agent rules", "operating principles", "repo rules", "constitution", "baseline rules", "operating law"], "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1028, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P01", "seq": "01", "name": "Harness Infrastructure Builder", "type": "SETUP", "class": "SETUP / INFRASTRUCTURE", "sprintRole": "Build the operational harness components that agents use to navigate, validate, and produce work in this repository", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "The governance contract (P00) exists but the repo lacks operational harness infrastructure: codebase maps, workflow specs, validators, artifact registries, hooks, skills, or operator reports.", "inspectFirst": "Existing AGENTS.md/governance contract, repo structure, test runners, validators, scripts, manifests, docs, generated-output policy, branch/PR conventions, any partial harness artifacts, and existing machine/profile/path/launcher/install/update conventions so a harness does not invent a competing repository location.", "expectedOutput": "Implemented and validated harness components committed to the repository: codebase map, workflow specs, artifact registry, validators, hooks (where useful), scoped skills, operator reports, and a machine-readable canonical development/production path contract or an explicit evidence-backed NOT-APPLICABLE disposition.", "nextStep": "After harness is built, run P11 (Validator) to prove the harness works end-to-end, or P12 (Closeout) to compress the handoff. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Each required harness component is tracked and validated; the harness resolves or explicitly dispositioned canonical development/production paths per supported machine/profile without inventing competing locations; remote integration is not promoted to local deployment proof; commit exists; push or PR state is reported.", "color": "Slate", "copySheet": "P01_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "BUILD THE REPO HARNESS NOW. DO NOT STOP AT A PLAN OR DESCRIPTION.\n\nRepo: resolve the current repository from the environment.\nBranch: preserve existing work; use the current safe branch or create an isolated feature branch.\nSprint: Harness Infrastructure Build.\nLane: harness infrastructure.\nOwned scope: harness component files (maps, workflows, validators, registries, hooks, skills, reports).\nForbidden scope: governance contract (that is P00 scope), product code changes, secrets, destructive cleanup.\nExpected artifacts: tracked harness component files, passing validators, commit SHA, push or PR evidence.\n\nWHAT THE HARNESS MUST INCLUDE\nA fresh agent entering this repo must be able to: inspect the harness, choose the right workflow, run validators, avoid known traps, produce artifacts, and hand off cleanly. Build these components:\n\n1. CODEBASE MAP\n   - Repository structure overview\n   - Key directories and their purposes\n   - Entry points and configuration files\n   - Build/test/deploy commands\n\n2. WORKFLOW SPECS\n   - How to pick up a task\n   - How to validate before committing\n   - How to handle failures\n   - How to hand off to the next agent or chat\n\n3. ARTIFACT REGISTRY\n   - What artifacts this repo produces\n   - Where they live\n   - How to generate them\n   - Naming conventions\n\n4. VALIDATORS\n   - Tests that prove correctness\n   - Lint/format checks\n   - Build verification\n   - Contract validators (schema, manifest, etc.)\n\n5. HOOKS (where useful)\n   - Pre-commit checks\n   - Pre-push validation\n   - File watchers or generators\n\n6. SCOPED SKILLS\n   - Repeatable procedures documented as skills\n   - Trigger conditions\n   - Required inputs and expected outputs\n\n7. OPERATOR REPORTS\n   - English-language summaries of repo state\n   - What is working, what is broken, what is missing\n   - Readable by humans, not just agents\n\n8. CANONICAL PATH CONTRACT\n   - Every app harness must answer where normal development occurs and where the app is actually used/installed/served in production for each supported machine/profile, or record why that distinction is not applicable.\n   - Reuse an existing path/profile registry when one exists. Otherwise add one machine-readable canonical owner and route agents, launchers, updaters, worktree helpers, and operator status through it.\n   - Distinguish canonical development checkout, production/use path, temporary worktree root, and real operator entrypoint. Do not let a fresh agent choose a new directory from model preference.\n   - Prevent path sprawl: a second mutable clone is not a substitute for the canonical checkout; preserve unique/dirty work and use approved isolated worktrees for parallel writers.\n   - Treat `remote main contains SHA`, `canonical development checkout is current`, `production/use path is current`, and `real entrypoint observes it` as different proof states. GitHub merge success alone is not workstation deployment proof.\n   - P92 Canonical Path Prompt owns deep repair/audit of this contract; P01 must ensure the harness has the seam so P92 has one canonical owner to inspect and strengthen.\n\n\nBUILD PROCEDURE\n1. Inspect: read governance contract (AGENTS.md), repo structure, existing tests/scripts/docs\n2. Identify which harness components are missing or incomplete\n3. Build each component as a tracked file in the appropriate location\n4. Run existing validators to ensure new components do not break anything\n5. Add a harness completeness check (test or script that verifies all components exist)\n6. Commit with message: feat(harness): build operational harness infrastructure\n7. Push and open or update a PR\n\nVALIDATE\n- Each harness component file exists and is tracked\n- Existing tests still pass\n- Harness completeness check passes\n- git diff --check passes\n- Commit SHA recorded\n- Push or PR evidence recorded\n\nFINAL REPORT\nRepo, branch, harness components built (list each), validator results, commit SHA, push or PR state, and one exact next command.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["harness builder", "build harness", "repo harness", "codebase map", "workflow", "validator", "artifact registry", "skills", "hooks", "operator reports", "infrastructure"], "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1029, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P02", "seq": "02", "name": "Previous Chat → Active Sprint Executor", "type": "HARVEST", "class": "CONTINUATION / EXECUTE", "sprintRole": "Recover prior-conversation intent and state, independently establish current reality, reconcile both evidence axes without letting either mask the other, then orient, summarize, close out, or continue through execution according to the operator's actual request.", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "A named previous conversation, handoff, or context boundary must be recovered for bearings, faithful summary, resumable closeout, or continued implementation. AUTO mode routes among ORIENT, SUMMARIZE, CLOSEOUT, and CONTINUE without forcing execution.", "inspectFirst": "Current request and supplied conversation/handoff; richest accessible transcript/history surfaces; then, when present truth matters, independently refresh repository/provider/runtime state, owning files, validators, artifacts, and blockers. Conversation intent and current reality are separate evidence axes until reconciliation.", "expectedOutput": "Mode-resolved continuity with an evidence-backed conversation-fidelity ledger, an independently established current-reality ledger when material, explicit reconciliation of confirmed/contradicted/unknown state, and then: bearings for ORIENT, faithful compression for SUMMARIZE, a resumable checkpoint for CLOSEOUT, or actual safe progress plus validation/integration evidence for CONTINUE. When meaningful unfinished requested work exists, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE also emit one compact execution-ready continuation capsule for the correct successor agent/provider surface without performing that work themselves. Multi-pass execution preserves compact in-flight evidence updates.", "nextStep": "Resolve MODE from the operator's words, recover conversation fidelity and current reality independently, run one bounded contradiction pass, then perform only the selected disposition. CONTINUE remains execution-first after alignment; ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT must not be silently promoted to implementation. In ORIENT or SUMMARIZE, unresolved requested work must still be converted into a compact successor handoff with the first executable action; non-execution constrains mutation, not continuation quality. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "P02 proves alignment structurally only when conversation fidelity and current reality are sourced independently before reconciliation; material claims preserve provenance and UNKNOWN state; later corrections/supersession are respected; ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT do not silently mutate or execute; ORIENT/SUMMARIZE emit a compact execution-ready successor handoff whenever meaningful unfinished requested work exists; `NEXT COMMAND: none` is reserved for no unfinished requested work or a genuine unresolved owner/dependency/user-only gate; CONTINUE still selects and executes the first safe unresolved slice; checkpoint freshness is revalidated; recursive P02 review is forbidden; and the focused P02 continuity regression matrix remains green. Recovery may use repository/provider reconstruction without a false blocker when current truth is sufficient; mutation authority is explicit before tracked-file, commit, push, PR, or merge actions. Repository/static proof does not prove downstream model obedience.", "color": "Gray", "copySheet": "P02_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "PREVIOUS CHAT:\nxyz_previous_chat_name\n\nMODE: AUTO | ORIENT | SUMMARIZE | CLOSEOUT | CONTINUE\n\nRECOVER THE PRIOR CONVERSATION FAITHFULLY, ESTABLISH CURRENT REALITY INDEPENDENTLY, RECONCILE THE TWO, THEN FOLLOW THE OPERATOR'S ACTUAL DISPOSITION.\n\nCONTEXT IS A STARTING MAP, NOT A SEARCH BOUNDARY.\n- do not make any one recovery surface a prerequisite for another.\n\nMISSION\nUse the named previous conversation as continuity evidence. Preserve decisions, corrections, rejected paths, proof, open work, and uncertainty that prevent rework. Independently refresh current repository/provider/runtime truth when it is material. Do not infer present truth from conversation history, and do not infer operator intent from repository state. Reconcile only after both axes are established.\n\nMODE ROUTING — WORDS MATTER\n- AUTO: infer the disposition from the operator's current request. Prefer the narrowest mode that fully satisfies it; do not turn a request to orient, summarize, or close out into implementation.\n- ORIENT: answer where things stand, what matters now, what changed, and what remains uncertain. No repository mutation merely to provide bearings.\n- SUMMARIZE: compress the conversation faithfully, preserving decisions, corrections, superseded state, unresolved disagreement, proof floors, and open questions. Do not execute work merely because open work exists.\n- CLOSEOUT: produce a resumable durable handoff/checkpoint with first unproven gate, exact next action, evidence anchors, blockers, and proof ceiling. Do not start a new implementation slice merely because one is available.\n- CONTINUE: CONTINUE THAT CHAT AS AN ACTIVE IMPLEMENTATION SPRINT. Recover and align first, then execute the highest-value safe unresolved slice. A short execution plan is allowed, but planning is subordinate to implementation.\n- If the operator explicitly says alternatives such as 'closeout or review', preserve the OR relationship. Do not silently rewrite alternatives as cumulative requirements.\n\n0. CONCISE PROGRESS LOOP\n- Do not work through multiple meaningful passes silently.\n- After each evidence-changing pass, or roughly every 2-3 tool groups during long execution, emit one compact update and continue.\n- Preferred shape: CHANGED: ... | PROVED: ... | NEXT: ...; use BLOCKED: ... only for a real blocker.\n- Prefer fragments over filler. Maximum two short sentences. Grammar polish is less important than signal.\n- Do not repeat the plan, narrate polling, or use an update as a stopping point while safe work remains in CONTINUE mode.\n- Before the final response, state pass count and fixed-point reason, or the exact blocker.\n- One trivial pass needs no mid-run update. Multi-pass silent execution fails this prompt.\n\n1. PIN THE CONTINUITY SUBJECT + SOURCE FLOOR\n- Bind the named conversation/handoff supplied by the operator. Start from current chat plus any pasted handoff, checkpoint, transcript excerpt, or prior summary.\n- Record the strongest accessible source surfaces and their limits. A summary is secondary evidence when richer transcript/history is available.\n- Do not ask the operator to repeat recoverable information.\n- Do not recursively invoke P02 from a P02 recovery/review lane. Parallel reviewers may inspect independent evidence, but P02 remains the single convergence owner.\n\n2. AXIS A — CONVERSATION FIDELITY\n- Recover what the operator actually said, decided, corrected, rejected, approved, completed, left open, or superseded.\n- SEGMENTED TRANSCRIPT MODE: when raw messages/pages/ranges/turns/timestamps/cursors exist, harvest materially relevant segments in bounded batches with small overlap where decisions cross boundaries.\n- For each segment capture decisions; implementation performed; open work; operator corrections; proof reached; proof gaps; owned/forbidden scope; repo/branch/PR/SHA/path/artifact identities; blockers; superseded state; proposed next actions; uncertainties.\n- Tag claims as FACT, OPERATOR INTENT, AGENT PROPOSAL, or UNKNOWN.\n- Reduce the segment records instead of concatenating summaries: normalize identities, deduplicate repetition, preserve chronology, resolve state transitions and later superseding decisions, and retain provenance for material claims.\n- A later decision in the transcript supersedes an earlier one when the evidence proves an actual state transition.\n- SEMANTIC-RETRIEVAL MODE: when raw transcript access is unavailable, perform multiple targeted retrievals rather than one broad query: decisions/constraints; unfinished work; identities; tests/proof/blockers; operator corrections/repeated failure modes; and next actions.\n- PROVIDED-CONTEXT MODE: a rich pasted handoff is sufficient to begin repository exploration and execution. Additional history may deepen or falsify it but cannot block repository/provider inspection while safe current evidence is available.\n\n3. AXIS B — CURRENT REALITY\n- VERIFY THE CURRENT FLOOR independently of Axis A whenever present truth matters to the requested mode.\n- Refresh repository/provider/runtime evidence: current default branch/head, branch/PR/worktree state, recent overlapping commits, checks/reviews, canonical owner files, validators, artifacts, deployments, and blockers as applicable. do not wait for historical retrieval to finish before exploring durable sources.\n- REPOSITORY RECONSTRUCTION FALLBACK: if history is unavailable, incomplete, compacted, or fails, reconstruct implementation state from current durable evidence without pretending that repository truth reveals unstated operator intent.\n- Loss of historical transcript or tool output is not itself a blocker when current evidence can reconstruct the material implementation state.\n- Preserve dirty or separately owned work. Do not revive work already merged, superseded, or proven complete merely because an old chat called it open.\n\n4. ALIGNMENT / RECONCILIATION\n- Only after both axes are independently established, classify material claims as CURRENT + CONFIRMED, CURRENT + UNVERIFIED, SUPERSEDED, HISTORICAL, CONTRADICTED, or UNKNOWN.\n- A passing conversation axis cannot erase a reality conflict. A current repository fact cannot erase unresolved operator intent.\n- Later explicit operator corrections supersede earlier intent when chronology proves a real decision change.\n- For consequential claims, verify the cited anchor before acting. Reviewer output is a hypothesis until its evidence is checked.\n- Run one deliberate contradiction/gap pass after the first reconciliation. Repeat only when the new pass changes material evidence. Do not review repeatedly until an agent happens to say 'clean'.\n\n4A. VALIDATED LIVE-THREAD CHECKPOINT MODE\n- When supplied context contains or points to a live-thread-p02-checkpoint/v1 payload, validate it with python scripts/validate_conversation_handoff_checkpoint.py <checkpoint.json> when repository execution exists.\n- Preserve settled decisions and valid historical proof. Refresh mutable evidence anchors before treating them as current truth.\n- Resume from each surviving thread's first_unproven_gate and next_action; do not replay completed work merely to rebuild context.\n- If validation fails, preserve supported fields, repair only invalid/missing state, and keep unsupported claims UNKNOWN.\n- Structural checkpoint validity proves resumability, not factual freshness.\n\n5. DISPOSITION\n- ORIENT: return current bearings, aligned state, meaningful changes, active risks, and unresolved unknowns. If meaningful unfinished requested work remains, append the compact non-execution continuation handoff in 5A. Stop when the user can accurately locate the work and the next capable owner can start without repeating orientation.\n- SUMMARIZE: return a faithful compressed record organized around decisions, corrections, state transitions, proof, unresolved work, and uncertainty. Preserve dissent/ambiguity instead of smoothing it away. If meaningful unfinished requested work remains, append the compact non-execution continuation handoff in 5A.\n- CLOSEOUT: emit or update the smallest durable resumable checkpoint/handoff appropriate to the environment. Preserve exact evidence anchors, completed proof, first unproven gate, next action, and return trigger. Close the conversation without inventing completion.\n- CONTINUE: proceed through sections 6-8 below. Do not stop at orientation or summary while safe executable work remains.\n\n\n5A. NON-EXECUTION CONTINUATION HANDOFF — ORIENT / SUMMARIZE\n- ORIENT and SUMMARIZE are non-mutating, not non-actionable. Their job is incomplete when they recover actionable unfinished work but make the successor rediscover it.\n- When meaningful unfinished requested work exists, emit exactly one compact execution-ready continuation capsule after the bearings/summary. Do not execute the capsule in ORIENT or SUMMARIZE.\n- Resolve the successor owner/surface from current evidence. For repository-local implementation, prefer the available/named local implementation agent or canonical repo executor; otherwise name the actual provider/agent owner. Do not invent an unavailable worker.\n- Reuse the smallest existing durable continuity owner (plan, PR, handoff, ledger, checkpoint, provider artifact) and point the successor to it. If none exists and the active non-execution mode forbids creating one, mark the capsule PROVISIONAL and name the intended durable owner; do not fabricate synchronization.\n- Keep the capsule small: at most 12 short bullets and normally no more than 700 words. Reference exact paths, PRs, SHAs, task IDs, artifacts, and durable plans instead of re-pasting the recovery narrative.\n- Required capsule fields: SUCCESSOR OWNER/SURFACE; CANONICAL CONTINUITY OWNER; MISSION; VERIFIED FLOOR; SETTLED / DO NOT REOPEN; FIRST UNPROVEN GATE; FIRST EXECUTABLE ACTION; OWNED SCOPE; FORBIDDEN SCOPE; EXPECTED ARTIFACT/PROOF; RETURN CONDITION.\n- Carry only decisions/corrections that prevent rework. Compress rejected or superseded paths to one-line do-not-reopen constraints when they materially protect the successor.\n- Do not emit a multi-chat launch pack from ORIENT/SUMMARIZE unless the operator explicitly asked for one or a real dependency graph makes a single successor packet unsafe. Default to the first dependency-ready execution lane.\n- NEXT COMMAND in ORIENT/SUMMARIZE is the first executable successor action, prefixed `HANDOFF ONLY — NOT EXECUTED IN THIS MODE:`, whenever unfinished work is actionable. Use `none; no actionable continuation remains` only when the requested outcome has no unfinished work, or an essential owner/dependency/operator-only decision is genuinely unresolved.\n\n6. SELECT THE FIRST UNFINISHED EXECUTION SLICE — CONTINUE ONLY\n- Identify what remains open after recovery and reconciliation.\n- Choose the highest-value safe item still genuinely unfinished and executable now.\n- State a compact sprint declaration: mission, lane, owned scope, forbidden scope, dependencies, collision risks, expected artifacts, validation, proof ceiling, and explicit mutation authority for tracked-file, commit, push, PR creation/update, merge action, deploy, or live-target actions.\n- Do not fan work into a multi-chat launch pack unless a real dependency makes immediate implementation unsafe.\n\n7. IMPLEMENT NOW — CONTINUE ONLY\n- Inspect owning files and existing repository patterns.\n- Modify useful tracked files or produce the owned artifact.\n- Add or repair tests, validators, registries, workflows, hooks, or docs when they enforce the selected behavior; do not replace deterministic application logic with prompt prose.\n- Generate canonical artifacts only through registered builders/producers.\n- Do not stop at a summary, TODO list, architecture discussion, branch listing, PR status, or plan while safe executable work remains.\n\n8. VALIDATE AND DELIVER — CONTINUE ONLY\n- Run focused tests/validators for the changed surface, required build/artifact parity checks, and git diff --check.\n- Run broader registered gates when repository contract requires them.\n- Commit coherent changes, push normally, and open/update/merge/deploy when safe, authorized, and required gates permit.\n- Preserve dependency order and concurrent work; never force-push or destructively clean unrelated state.\n- Do not claim a higher proof level than actually observed.\n\nFINAL RESPONSE\nMode resolved: <AUTO -> ORIENT | SUMMARIZE | CLOSEOUT | CONTINUE, with the evidence for that routing>\nPrevious chat resolved: <name and whether retrieval succeeded, including recovery mode>\nConversation fidelity: <decisions, corrections, superseded state, unresolved intent>\nCurrent reality: <current verified state, or not required/not accessible>\nAlignment: <confirmed, contradicted, unknown, and the consequences>\nRecovery ledger: <material decisions, superseded state, proof floor, unresolved unknowns>\nRemaining gaps: <only still-open items>\nDisposition result: <bearings | summary | checkpoint/handoff | executed sprint>\nFor CONTINUE also report repo/branch/PR, changes, files/artifacts, validation, commit, push/PR/merge/deploy state, proof ceiling, remaining gaps, and Git state.\nNEXT COMMAND: one exact executable action that advances the next unproven state in CONTINUE/CLOSEOUT. For ORIENT/SUMMARIZE with actionable unfinished work, emit the first successor action as `HANDOFF ONLY — NOT EXECUTED IN THIS MODE: <action>`; use `none; no actionable continuation remains` only when no unfinished requested work remains or an essential owner/dependency/operator-only decision is genuinely unresolved.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["previous chat", "continue chat", "resume conversation", "chat continuation", "new chat sprint", "unfinished chat", "implement previous chat", "sprint upon context", "conversation context", "continue implementation", "conversation transcript", "transcript recovery", "segmented transcript", "history unavailable", "handoff recovery", "repository reconstruction", "orient previous chat", "summarize chat", "chat summary", "closeout chat", "conversation closeout", "conversation alignment", "continuity mode", "current reality", "conversation fidelity", "resumable checkpoint"], "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1030, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P03", "seq": "03", "name": "Repository Evidence + First Sprint Executor", "type": "DISCOVERY + BUILD", "class": "REPO / EVIDENCE + ACTION", "sprintRole": "Analyze the repo and execute the first safe sprint", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "Entering an unfamiliar, stale, or context-heavy repo. This includes creating a greenfield repository that needs its first bounded architecture/build decision.", "inspectFirst": "Git floor, rules, architecture, manifests, PRs, plans, tests, validators, artifacts. For greenfield work, also recover the product outcome, credible scale/concurrency envelope, availability needs, operator tolerance, and budget before scaffolding.", "expectedOutput": "Cited evidence pack, ranked queue, and first bounded repo mutation, plus explicit closeout of remaining gaps, risks, blockers, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation.", "nextStep": "Use P11 or P12. Report the current gap/risk/blocker and then execute the first safe dependency-aware continuation; do not stop at status-only reporting while agent-capable work remains. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Path-grounded evidence plus actual repo movement. Closeout is incomplete while a known in-scope gap, risk, blocker, proof limitation, or executable continuation is omitted.", "color": "Sky", "copySheet": "P03_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "ANALYZE THE REPOSITORY, BUILD THE EVIDENCE PACK, THEN EXECUTE THE FIRST SAFE SPRINT. DO NOT STOP AT A PLAN.\nRepo: xyz_repo_or_path\nBranch / PR if known: xyz_branch\nContext or plan path if available: xyz_plan_directory\nMISSION\nRecover operational truth from the repository itself, derive a ranked sprint queue from evidence, and immediately execute the highest-value bounded sprint that is safe in the current environment.\nTreat the current repository as the source of truth over remembered chat context.\nCollect all actionable evidence, not every byte or generated dependency.\nDO NOT ANSWER WITH\n- a repo summary only\n- a plan only\n- a prompt pack only\n- generic best practices without file-path evidence\n- a request for permission when a safe bounded sprint is available\n- confidence based only on filenames, timestamps, or stale handoff prose\nCOMPACT PREFLIGHT\nRun what is available and record exact skips:\ngit rev-parse --show-toplevel\ngit status --short\ngit branch --show-current\ngit log --oneline --decorate -12\ngit worktree list\ngit remote -v\ngh pr list --state open --limit 30\ngh pr status\nIf the worktree is dirty or conflicted:\n- preserve existing work\n- identify the owning branch, files, and likely sprint\n- use or create an isolated worktree when this lane does not own the dirt\n- make repo-floor repair the first sprint only when it is the safest useful move\n- otherwise stop with the exact blocker and safest next command\nREPOSITORY EVIDENCE HARVEST\nInspect the repository before selecting work:\n- local operating law: AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .ai/, skills, workflow rules, CONTRIBUTING, README\n- architecture and entry points: source roots, services, launchers, apps, packages, modules\n- manifests and toolchain: build files, dependency files, task runners, lockfiles, environment contracts\n- recent center of gravity: recent commits, changed files, active branches, open PRs, review comments when available\n- plans and state: docs, status files, roadmaps, handoffs, decision logs, release notes\n- validation surface: tests, validators, linters, type checks, builds, CI workflows, fixtures, smoke scripts\n- harness spine: run context, artifact registry, schemas, workflow specs, reports, hooks, codebase maps\n- agent leverage: repo-local prompts, scoped skills, MCP/LSP or read-only navigation helpers\n- artifacts and output policy: generated files, logs, reports, ignored paths, committed evidence, cleanup rules\n- unresolved signals: TODO, FIXME, XXX, NotImplemented, skipped tests, quarantines, stubs, failing checks\n- runtime and deployment seams: launch scripts, installers, browser flows, target mutation boundaries, safety docs\n- stale or conflicting context: placeholder plans, superseded docs, abandoned branches, contradictory instructions\nEVIDENCE RULES\n- cite exact file paths, commands, commit SHAs, PR numbers, and validator names\n- distinguish FACT, INFERENCE, and UNKNOWN\n- use document content and git history to judge freshness; timestamps alone are weak evidence\n- search existing contracts, helpers, names, scripts, and output patterns before inventing\n- do not expose secrets, credentials, personal data, or huge generated logs\n- do not crawl vendored dependencies or build outputs unless they are directly relevant\n- name unavailable tools and the exact command that should be run later\nBUILD THE REPO EVIDENCE PACK\nProduce a compact operational ledger with these sections:\n1. REPO IDENTITY\n- repo path, branch, HEAD, remotes, worktrees, dirty/conflicted state, open PRs\n2. CURRENT CENTER OF GRAVITY\n- what recent commits and changed files show the repo is actively becoming\n3. ACTIVE WORKSTREAMS\n- feature, harness, cleanup, validation, docs/reporting, runtime proof, release/PR hygiene, research/design\n4. HARNESS INVENTORY\n- present, partial, missing, duplicated, or stale: rules, map, workflows, run context, registry, validators, hooks, skills, reports\n5. PRODUCT / FEATURE INVENTORY\n- implemented surfaces, incomplete surfaces, entry points, and integration boundaries\n6. VALIDATION AND PROOF SURFACE\n- commands, CI jobs, fixtures, artifacts, and the highest proof each can support\n7. UNRESOLVED SIGNALS\n- TODOs, failing or skipped checks, placeholders, stale plans, review requests, unmerged useful work\n8. RISKS AND FORBIDDEN SCOPE\n- mutation risks, credentials, live targets, destructive operations, unrelated rewrite zones\n9. IMPORTANT PATHS\n- the small set of files and directories the next action depends on\nDERIVE THE SPRINT QUEUE\nCreate a ranked list of bounded sprint candidates.\nFor each candidate include:\n- title and lane\n- evidence and why now\n- owned scope and forbidden scope\n- dependencies and collision risks\n- files to inspect and files likely to change\n- expected artifacts\n- validation commands\n- risk and proof ceiling\n- whether it is safe in parallel\n\nGREENFIELD REPOSITORY CREATION\nWhen the repository does not exist yet or is only an empty shell, recover the product outcome and credible scale envelope before scaffolding. If module boundaries or deployment topology are materially uncertain, route that bounded design decision to P95. Do not make Docker, Podman, Kubernetes, microservices, or a managed platform mandatory merely because future scale is possible. Create the smallest skeleton consistent with the decision; P00 remains governance owner and P01 remains harness plus canonical build/test/deploy/path owner.\n\nFIRST-SPRINT SELECTION RULES\n- floor before furniture: repair unsafe repo state before feature work\n- shared contracts before reports, dashboards, MCP consumers, or duplicated output shapes\n- unblock the highest number of later sprints with the smallest bounded change\n- prefer a real tested slice over broad documentation or speculative refactoring\n- do not misclassify evidenced structural editability debt as speculative refactoring: when tangled, duplicated, mixed-responsibility, or oversized source materially raises change risk for humans or agents, route a bounded cleanup to P124 rather than letting green behavior hide the debt\n- do not select live mutation or runtime proof when the environment or scope forbids it\nEXECUTE THE FIRST SAFE SPRINT\nState repo, branch, PR/sprint, lane, owned scope, forbidden scope, and expected artifacts.\nThen immediately do one of these:\n1. Modify useful tracked repo files within the selected sprint.\n2. Prove the selected sprint is already complete with committed files and passing checks.\n3. Stop with an exact blocker plus the smallest patch, diff, or file content that should be applied.\nDefault to option 1.\nWork rules:\n- reuse repository patterns\n- keep changes bounded\n- avoid unrelated rewrites\n- add or update tests, validators, scripts, manifests, or operational docs that enforce the behavior\n- do not create a tracked census document merely to narrate the scan\n- persist a repo-intake artifact only when repo conventions or downstream workflows need it\n- keep generated evidence in approved ignored paths unless sanitized tracked evidence is explicitly required\nVALIDATE AND DELIVER\nRun targeted tests, relevant validators/static checks, build checks, and broader checks when practical.\nRun git diff --check, git status --short, git diff --stat, and review the final diff.\nCommit and push or open/update a PR when the environment allows.\nDo not claim a higher proof level than the evidence supports.\nFINAL RESPONSE FORMAT\nREPO EVIDENCE\n- identity, center of gravity, active workstreams, harness gaps, important paths\nSPRINT QUEUE\n- ranked candidates and the first-sprint decision\nWORK COMMITTED\n- summary, files changed, artifacts, commit SHA, push/PR state\nVALIDATION\n- commands, results, skipped checks, proof level reached\nGAPS / RISKS\n- blockers, unknowns, forbidden scope, collision risks\nFINAL GIT STATE\n- git status --short\nNEXT COMMAND\n- one exact command\nDo not include a next-agent prompt. Use P12 only after the repository-derived sprint is complete.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- A gap, risk, or blocker must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps instead of merely listing them.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, remaining gaps/risks are either closed or explicitly accepted by scope, and no safe unproven action remains.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["discovery", "enter repo", "unfamiliar repo", "new repo", "stale repo", "evidence pack", "first sprint", "first commit", "repo analysis", "greenfield repository", "create repository", "repository creation", "architecture-first repo"], "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 3, "discoveryGroup": "promoted", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P04", "seq": "04", "name": "[PARALLEL] Repo-Aware Sprint + Harness Factoring Distributor", "type": "PLAN", "class": "PLAN / DISTRIBUTE", "sprintRole": "Factor remaining work into dependency-aware lanes and produce an automation-ready parallel dispatch manifest with explicit convergence ownership", "progress": "No", "useWhen": "Repo state is known but harness, agent, and application work need explicit factoring and ownership.", "inspectFirst": "P03 evidence or current repo state.", "expectedOutput": "A durable factoring plan whose primary orchestration artifact is a PARALLEL DISPATCH MANIFEST: dependency graph, graph width, collision ownership, execution-adapter ladder resolution, executable launch actions, validation/proof gates, and convergence owner. Human copy panels are portability fallback only, not the primary scheduler. The dispatch manifest is a real JSON artifact validated against the repository contract, not prose-only planning. Runtime partition assigns host execution environment and provider access before adapter selection; sanitized typed evidence_inputs are required in the dispatch manifest.", "nextStep": "Resolve and reuse the canonical repository plan/handoff owner or a relevant writable active PR owned by this lane. If none exists and repository mutation is authorized, create the smallest tracked plan artifact following existing repository plan/docs/harness conventions; persist the complete accepted factoring plan and PARALLEL DISPATCH MANIFEST there and index it from P66/the work ledger when present. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use or create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead. If the planning task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL and name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED. Route execution to P07 from the validated manifest. If graph width is at least two, every dependency-ready lane must have an autonomous execution adapter or an explicit AUTONOMY_GAP with a machine-executable bootstrap/repair owner; do not make the operator launch chats. Validate the JSON manifest with `scripts/prompt_parallel_dispatch.py` before P07 consumes it. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Dependencies, graph width, collision ownership, adapter choice, executable launch action, return contract, successor phases, convergence owner, and proof gates are explicit for every lane; the plan is durable; no dependency-ready parallel lane silently depends on human chat creation when an autonomous adapter exists; all-rungs failure is recorded as DEGRADED plus AUTONOMY_GAP rather than clean serial completion. The JSON manifest validates through `scripts/prompt_parallel_dispatch.py`; command-addressable lanes are actually launched from it or runtime-tool lanes return separately verifiable tool/API receipts. Runtime placement uses the shared planning.runtime_partition contract; LAUNCH ORDER remains first; private provider IDs are absent from tracked manifests; durable plan synchronization is reflected in the relevant writable active PR when this lane owns it.", "color": "Amber", "copySheet": "P04_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "You are entering an existing repository with prior chat context and need to factor the remaining work into bounded sprints.\nRepo:\n`xyz_repo_or_path`\nPlan or context path if available:\n`xyz_plan_directory`\nIf the plan path is missing, stale, blank, or placeholder, do not stall.\nInspect the repository, current branch, recent commits, open PRs, worktrees, status docs, handoffs, plans, tests, validators, workflows, artifacts, skills, capabilities, triggers, and current git state.\nPrefer current repository evidence over remembered context.\nCOMPACT PREFLIGHT\nRecord:\n- repo\n- primary local path if known\n- branch\n- head\n- status\n- worktrees\n- open PRs\n- recent center of gravity\n- likely active sprint\n- stale or missing context\nInspect before distributing work:\n- recent commits and changed files\n- open PR diffs, checks, reviews, and dependencies\n- docs, plans, handoffs, and codebase maps\n- schemas, run context, artifact registry, validators, and fixtures\n- workflows, launchers, and runtime evidence\n- agent skills, capabilities, triggers, routing, manifests, MCP/tools, and hooks\n- application services, state machines, adapters, persistence, UI, launchers, and deployment logic\nRUNTIME PARTITION / EXECUTION PLACEMENT — REQUIRED\nBefore FACTORING PASS, apply the shared planning.runtime_partition contract (harness/contracts/planning-runtime-partition.v1.json; scripts/planning_runtime_partition.py): choose exactly one HOST (CURRENT_CHAT_RUNTIME|LOCAL_AGENT_RUNTIME|CI_OR_REMOTE_RUNNER|OPERATOR_OR_PHYSICAL_RUNTIME|UNKNOWN_RUNTIME) plus zero or more PROVIDER routes. Providers are not hosts. Keep ChatGPT+provider work on CURRENT_CHAT_RUNTIME; never punt current-runtime work to local agents; never assign local FS/shell work to CURRENT_CHAT_RUNTIME; inherit sanitized evidence (type/owner/opaque ref/freshness/proof ceiling); never track private provider IDs; P07 retains implementation ownership; partition precedes adapter selection. Internal stage only: LAUNCH ORDER stays first; emit placement table after it. Manifest lanes require execution_environment, provider_access, required_capabilities, typed evidence_inputs.\nFACTORING PASS\nClassify each topic into one primary ownership surface:\n- floor or cleanup\n- harness spine\n- agent harness\n- conventional application logic\n- integration seam\n- validation\n- docs/reporting\n- runtime proof\n- research/design\n- unsafe or blocked\nFor agent-harness surfaces, inventory every relevant skill, capability, and trigger.\nFor each, determine whether to keep, create, split, merge, retire, or rewire it.\nRequire deterministic activation conditions, explicit inputs and outputs, preconditions, guardrails, owner, tests, and proof ceiling.\nUse these boundaries:\n- skills are reusable workflow guidance\n- capabilities are reusable operations or tool-backed actions\n- triggers route deterministic conditions to a skill, capability, or workflow\n- product and domain behavior belongs in application code and contracts\n- prompts must not become the only implementation of application logic\nSeparate harness factoring from application-logic factoring when they own different files or proof gates.\nDetect shared-file, shared-schema, shared-workflow, skill, capability, trigger, and PR-stacking collisions before recommending parallel work.\nPARALLEL CAPABILITY LADDER / AUTONOMY GATE\nParallelism is a requirement of the dependency graph, not a feature of one preferred worker product.\n- First build the owned dependency graph. If at least two meaningful dependency-ready lanes can proceed without conflicting writes, graph width is at least two and parallel dispatch remains required.\n- Probe execution adapters in order, using only mechanisms actually evidenced in the current environment: (1) native sub-agent/child-agent/delegated-agent/task-worker APIs; (2) repository/local agent runners and orchestrators such as repo-proven AgentSwitchboard, GNHF, OpenCode, Cursor/Codex/Claude wrappers; (3) connected remote/provider/MCP execution surfaces that can independently own bounded lanes; (4) CI/job/matrix fan-out capable of executing the lane safely; (5) genuinely concurrent local processes/tool jobs for deterministic non-LLM lanes. Do not invent an adapter merely because it appears in this list.\n- Absence of one adapter class is not global absence. In particular, `no connected self-hosted workers` rules out only that adapter class; continue down the capability ladder before declaring degraded execution.\n- At the first safe rung with enough capacity for at least two dependency-ready lanes, dispatch immediately. Record adapter, lane identity, mutation owner, launch action, return artifact/proof, and convergence owner.\n- Serial calls to multiple tools are not parallelism. A tool/process rung counts only when independent jobs are actually launched concurrently and their results are rejoined.\n- If graph width is one after real dependency/collision analysis, report `PARALLEL EXECUTION: NOT_APPLICABLE — dependency graph width is 1.` Serial execution is then correct, not degraded.\n- If graph width is at least two but every safe rung is genuinely unavailable or blocked, serial progress may continue only to avoid deadlock. Report `PARALLEL EXECUTION: DEGRADED — <exhausted capability-ladder evidence>.` and `AUTONOMY_GAP: <smallest executable adapter/bootstrap/repair route>.` Parallel-execution proof remains UNPROVEN; do not convert degraded serial progress into a clean parallel pass.\n- Never make the operator create chats, paste lane prompts, shuttle context, or act as the scheduler when any autonomous adapter can carry the lane. Copyable panels are machine-readable transport that agents must ingest and dispatch; human paste is incidental compatibility.\n- When the owned scope includes the missing adapter seam, build or repair the smallest durable adapter now. Otherwise route the AUTONOMY_GAP to its canonical owner as a machine-executable follow-on without blocking independent safe progress.\n\nPARALLEL DISPATCH MANIFEST\nFor every lane record: lane_id; mission; dependencies; owned mutation surfaces; forbidden surfaces; branch/worktree or read-only posture; selected execution adapter and ladder rung; exact launch action/tool/API/workflow; expected artifact/return contract; validation; convergence owner; and status. The manifest is the primary orchestration artifact. Copyable chat panels are machine-ingestible transport for portability and agent execution; a plan that requires the operator to launch dependency-ready lanes manually is not automation-complete.\n\nEXECUTABLE MANIFEST CONTRACT\n- The primary dispatch artifact is JSON at `Outputs/prompt-parallel-dispatch/manifest.json` conforming to `harness/contracts/prompt-parallel-dispatch.v1.json`; prose, a lane table, or copy panels alone do not satisfy this gate.\n- Validate it before dispatch: `python scripts/prompt_parallel_dispatch.py validate --manifest Outputs/prompt-parallel-dispatch/manifest.json`.\n- For command-addressable `argv` lanes, execute the manifest rather than narrating it: `python scripts/prompt_parallel_dispatch.py run --manifest Outputs/prompt-parallel-dispatch/manifest.json --receipt Outputs/prompt-parallel-dispatch/receipt.json`, then verify with `python scripts/prompt_parallel_dispatch.py verify-receipt --manifest Outputs/prompt-parallel-dispatch/manifest.json --receipt Outputs/prompt-parallel-dispatch/receipt.json`.\n- For `runtime_tool` lanes, the active agent runtime must consume the typed tool/operation/arguments records, perform the actual tool/API calls, preserve returned evidence in a compatible receipt, and run `verify-receipt`. The repository CLI must not impersonate an unavailable runtime tool.\n- Validation without launch is not dispatch proof. A REQUIRED width >= 2 receipt must prove `observed_parallelism=true`; DEGRADED execution may never do so.\n\nOUTPUT ORDER — LAUNCH ORDER FIRST\nAnalyze fully, then output in this order:\n1. LAUNCH ORDER\nPut the launch order at the very top.\nName the first chat, proof gate, parallel groups, waiting lanes, and final convergence.\nNumber and title the panels exactly as they will appear.\nDisplay the panels below in that exact order.\nNever use a different display order, category order, or convenience order.\nKeep every parallel group contiguous.\nCopy-panel portability fallback only: one panel maps to one lane when no autonomous adapter can carry that lane. This is not the primary dispatch path.\n2. PARALLEL DISPATCH MANIFEST\nEmit the machine-executable JSON dispatch manifest as the primary orchestration artifact before portability panels.\nValidate it with `scripts/prompt_parallel_dispatch.py validate` when mutation authority allows writing the artifact.\nCopyable chat panels remain portability/recovery fallback only.\n3. COMPACT COORDINATION PREAMBLE\nInclude repo floor, waves, hard dependencies, safe parallel lanes, collision owners, safety boundary, and proof ceiling.\nThe preamble is not a separately copied prompt fragment.\nDo not output a shared preamble that the operator must paste above each sprint.\n4. PORTABILITY FALLBACK SPRINT PANELS\nAfter the dispatch manifest, emit each complete lane prompt in its own separate writing block or copyable panel strictly as a portability/recovery fallback.\nEach panel must have its own copy control.\nPanels must appear in the exact launch order listed above.\nDo not place all prompts in one code fence.\nDo not place two prompts in one panel.\nDo not make the operator copy global rules from another section.\nEach panel must be self-contained and include:\n- banner and sprint name\n- repo, local path if known, branch, PR, wave, and lane\n- hard dependencies and safe parallel work\n- owned scope and forbidden scope\n- expected artifacts\n- mission\n- read-first files\n- compact preflight\n- numbered tasks\n- safety\n- validation order\n- commit, push, and PR contract when mutation is intended\n- proof level and proof ceiling\n- exact final response contract\n- exact next command\nPut only a short sprint title above each panel and no commentary between panels.\n5. SUPPORTING FACTORING LEDGER\nAfter the panels, provide:\nTOPICS FOUND\nClassify feature, harness, agent harness, application logic, integration, cleanup, validation, docs/reporting, runtime proof, release/PR hygiene, research/design, and blocked topics.\nHARNESS FACTORING\nList run context, artifact registry, schemas, validators, fixtures, workflows, skills, capabilities, triggers, routing, instruction surfaces, manifests, MCP/tools, hooks, and ownership.\nAPPLICATION LOGIC FACTORING\nList domain services, state machines, adapters, persistence, UI, launchers, jobs, deployment orchestration, and ownership.\nSPRINT CANDIDATES\nFor each include title, goal, why now, owned scope, forbidden scope, files to inspect, files likely to change, expected outputs, validation, dependencies, parallel safety, risk level, and proof ceiling.\nRECOMMENDED EXECUTION ORDER\nConfirm panels match launch order; keep floor/shared contracts before consumers.\nQUALITY RULES\nBe ambitious but practical.\nAvoid permission theater, duplicate ownership, giant prompts, and trivial-only progress.\nDo not claim higher proof from lower proof.\n\nDURABLE PLAN OUTPUT\n- Chat is not the sole owner of an actionable plan; persist the complete map (floor, successors, parallel groups, collisions, scope, artifacts, gates, proof ceiling, deferred work) to the canonical plan/spec/handoff path or writable owned PR.\n- If no owner exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact before routing to execution. Use P66/ledger as index only. Unrelated PRs are not write targets; read-only scope makes durability BLOCKED.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["plan", "distribute", "factor", "sprint lanes", "work lanes", "harness factoring", "ordering", "dependencies", "parallel lanes", "distributed", "concurrent agents"], "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 17, "discoveryGroup": "promoted", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P05", "seq": "05", "name": "[SERIALIZED] Ordered Sprint Plan Pack Generator", "type": "PLAN", "class": "PLAN / PACK", "sprintRole": "Generate a multi-chat launch pack", "progress": "No", "useWhen": "Multiple agents or sequential chats need one-click launch panels, bounded factoring, and proof rules.", "inspectFirst": "Repo evidence, candidate lanes, branch/PR state.", "expectedOutput": "Launch order at the top, compact coordination preamble, then one self-contained copy panel per sprint in that exact order. Runtime partition uses the shared planning.runtime_partition contract; placement table follows LAUNCH ORDER; successor panels carry sanitized inherited evidence.", "nextStep": "Launch P06/P07/P08 as required. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Every lane is independently copyable; panel display equals launch order; no shared-preamble assembly. Shared runtime partition precedes ordered-pack construction without weakening launch-order-first or inventing local sprints for already-executed current-runtime work.", "color": "Amber", "copySheet": "P05_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "Create a sprint plan pack for parallel or sequential AI chats.\nOPERATING RULE\nOne sprint panel goes into one new chat.\nDo not paste multiple sprint panels into the same chat.\nBuild the pack from the best available context in this chat and, when repo access exists, from current repository evidence.\nPrefer current repo, branch, PR, worktree, test, validator, workflow, artifact, skill, capability, trigger, and CI state over remembered context.\nOUTPUT PRESENTATION CONTRACT\nThe first substantive section must be LAUNCH ORDER.\nDo not begin with a shared copyable preamble.\nDo not require the operator to assemble a prompt from multiple boxes.\nEvery sprint panel must be self-contained.\n1. LAUNCH ORDER — PUT THIS AT THE TOP\nState the first chat to launch, why it is first, the exact proof gate that unlocks later work, every parallel group, waiting lanes, and final convergence.\nNumber and title every sprint exactly as it will appear below.\nThe sprint panels must be displayed in the exact same order as this launch order.\nDo not present panels in category order, numeric convenience order, or any order different from the recommended launch order.\nDo not offer an alternate display order.\nKeep prompts in each parallel group contiguous and label the group once.\nDo not repeat a conflicting launch order at the bottom.\n2. COMPACT COORDINATION PREAMBLE\nAfter the launch order, provide one short readable preamble in normal markdown.\nInclude:\n- repo\n- primary local path if known\n- current branch, main SHA, active PR, or verified floor when known\n- first commands to inspect state\n- dirty or conflicted worktree rule\n- worktree policy\n- proof requirements\n- what must not be committed\n- safety constraints\n- branch and PR expectations\n- wave order\n- hard dependencies\n- safe parallel lanes\n- collision risks and ownership\nKeep this preamble compact enough to scan without repeated scrolling.\nThe preamble is orientation only and is not copied separately into sprint chats.\nEvery panel below repeats the essential rules it needs.\nRUNTIME PARTITION / EXECUTION PLACEMENT — REQUIRED\nBefore pack construction, apply the same shared planning.runtime_partition contract as P04 (harness/contracts/planning-runtime-partition.v1.json; scripts/planning_runtime_partition.py): one HOST plus zero or more PROVIDER routes. Serialized ≠ local. Complete safe current-runtime work first; ChatGPT+provider stays CURRENT_CHAT_RUNTIME; do not invent local sprints for already-executed work—pass sanitized evidence forward; never assign FS/shell-only work to CURRENT_CHAT_RUNTIME; P07 owns implementation; never track private provider IDs. LAUNCH ORDER remains first substantive section; placement table follows it. Successor panels include EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT, PROVIDER / ACCESS ROUTE, REQUIRED CAPABILITIES, INHERITED EVIDENCE, RUNTIME HANDOFF.\n3. FACTORING PASS\nBefore creating sprint panels, factor the work into these ownership surfaces:\n- floor and cleanup\n- harness spine\n- agent harness\n- conventional application logic\n- integration seams\n- validation\n- docs/reporting\n- runtime proof\n- research/design\nFor the harness spine, identify run context, artifact registry, schemas, validators, fixtures, workflows, proof gates, and shared output contracts.\nFor the agent harness, identify skills, capabilities, triggers, routing, instruction surfaces, manifests, MCP/tools, hooks, and context injection.\nFor conventional application logic, identify domain models, services, state machines, adapters, persistence, UI, launchers, jobs, and deployment orchestration.\nFor every skill, capability, or trigger, decide whether to keep, create, split, merge, retire, or rewire it.\nRecord:\n- canonical owner\n- deterministic activation condition\n- inputs and outputs\n- preconditions and forbidden conditions\n- guardrails\n- tests\n- proof level\n- files and manifests likely to change\nUse these boundaries:\n- skills describe reusable workflow guidance\n- capabilities expose reusable operations or tool-backed actions\n- triggers deterministically route conditions to a skill, capability, or workflow\n- application and domain behavior remains in code and contracts\n- prompts may orchestrate work but must not become the only implementation of product logic\nCreate separate factoring sprints when agent harness work and application logic own different files, contracts, or proof gates.\n4. PROOF TAXONOMY\nDefine only the proof levels relevant to the application, including when applicable:\n- contract proof\n- harness proof\n- static test proof\n- build proof\n- launcher or browser proof\n- command ACK proof\n- movement or behavior observed proof\n- live runtime proof\n- deployment completion proof\n- operator acceptance proof\nState clearly: do not claim a higher proof level from a lower one.\nInclude concrete non-equivalences such as unit PASS not equaling launcher proof, command ACK not equaling observed behavior, and fixture E2E not equaling live runtime proof.\n5. WAVES, DEPENDENCIES, AND COLLISIONS\nGroup work into:\n- Wave 0: clean or prove the floor\n- Wave A: harness spine\n- Wave B: conventional application and product reliability\n- Wave C: agent harness leverage\n- Wave D: integration and cleanup\nUse the floor-before-furniture rule:\nReports, dashboards, catalogs, MCP, skills, agents, and UI layers may not invent output shapes before canonical run context and artifact registry contracts exist.\nName which sprint establishes the floor and which later sprints must wait.\nName:\n- hard dependencies\n- soft dependencies\n- safe parallel lanes\n- shared-file collision risks\n- shared-schema collision risks\n- shared-workflow collision risks\n- skill, capability, and trigger ownership collisions\n- branch and PR stacking risks\n- the single owner for each shared surface\nDo not assign the same shared file, schema, workflow, skill, capability, or trigger to multiple simultaneous sprint owners.\n6. SPRINT PANELS\nImmediately after the preamble, output every sprint as its own separate writing block or copyable panel.\nEach panel must have its own copy control.\nOrder the panels in the exact launch order stated at the top.\nDo not put all sprint prompts in one giant code fence.\nDo not put two sprint prompts in one panel.\nDo not place long analysis between sprint panels.\nDo not require the operator to combine text from multiple sections.\nPut only a short sprint title immediately above each panel.\nEvery panel must contain the complete prompt to paste into one new AI chat.\nEvery sprint panel must include:\nBANNER\nSPRINT NAME\nREPO\nPRIMARY LOCAL PATH\nBRANCH\nPR, IF KNOWN\nWAVE\nLANE\nDEPENDENCIES\nSAFE PARALLEL WORK\nOWNED SCOPE\nFORBIDDEN SCOPE\nEXPECTED ARTIFACTS\nMISSION\nREAD FIRST\nCOMPACT PREFLIGHT\nDIRTY WORKTREE RULE\nTASKS\nSAFETY\nVALIDATION\nCOMMIT AND PUSH CONTRACT\nPROOF CONTRACT\nFINAL RESPONSE CONTRACT\nEXACT NEXT COMMAND\nCOMPACT PREFLIGHT must include at minimum:\ngit rev-parse --show-toplevel\ngit status --short\ngit branch --show-current\ngit log --oneline --decorate -12\ngit worktree list\ngit remote -v\ngit fetch --all --prune\ngh pr list --state open --limit 50\ngh pr status\nAdd exact PR inspection commands when relevant.\nDIRTY WORKTREE RULE must require preserving unknown work, avoiding destructive cleanup, and using an isolated worktree when the sprint does not own the dirt.\nTASKS must be numbered and name exact files, contracts, scripts, validators, tests, skills, capabilities, triggers, workflows, and artifacts when evidence supports them.\nVALIDATION must list targeted tests, validators, static checks, build or launcher checks, broader regressions, git diff and status checks, and final CI in execution order.\nCOMMIT AND PUSH CONTRACT must require:\ngit diff --check\ngit status --short\ngit diff --stat\ngit diff\ngit add <changed tracked files>\ngit commit -m \"<useful message>\"\ngit push -u origin <branch>\nRequire opening or updating the correct PR when appropriate.\nPROOF CONTRACT must require proof level reached, supporting evidence, proof ceiling, and missing live or operator evidence.\nFINAL RESPONSE CONTRACT must require:\nCONTEXT\nCOMPLETED WORK\nVALIDATION\nGENERATED ARTIFACTS\nKNOWN GAPS / RISKS\nSKIPPED CHECKS\nFINAL GIT STATE\nNEXT COMMAND\nNEXT-AGENT HANDOFF\nRequire changed files, commits, PR URL, validation output, artifact paths, git status, skipped checks with exact later commands, and the next bounded owner.\n7. EXECUTION REQUIREMENT\nEvery sprint panel must tell the new chat to execute repository work and not stop at a plan.\nRequire at least one useful repository or GitHub mutation unless the sprint is already proven complete or an exact blocker prevents safe mutation.\nA valid result must include changed tracked files, a commit SHA, push or PR mutation, branch or worktree mutation, merge or cherry-pick evidence, or an exact blocker with the smallest useful patch or diff.\n8. SUPPORTING LEDGER\nAfter all panels, include the factoring ledger, dependency map, collision ledger, proof taxonomy, and supporting rationale.\nThis ledger may explain the launch order but may not change it.\nDo not add a second alternative order.\nFINAL QUALITY RULES\nMake the pack practical, bold, compact, and executable.\nOptimize for one-click copying, minimal scrolling, self-contained prompts, explicit ownership, and clean agent handoff.\nDo not optimize only for reading.\nDo not output one monolithic markdown document containing all sprint prompts in one code fence.\nDo not hide operational instructions outside the sprint panels.\nDo not allow skills, capabilities, triggers, or application logic to remain unfactored when they create separate ownership or proof requirements.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["launch pack", "multi chat", "sprint plan", "copy panel", "one click", "writing panel", "parallel lanes", "sequential", "serialized", "ordered steps"], "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 18, "discoveryGroup": "promoted", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P06", "seq": "06", "name": "Repo / PR Cleanup Executor", "type": "CLEANUP", "class": "REPO / PR CLEANUP", "sprintRole": "Clean repo and PR state with preservation-first authority", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "The user wants cherry-picks, repairs, merges, comments, branch mutations, and PR closure.", "inspectFirst": "Branches, worktrees, open PRs, unique commits, checks, dependencies.", "expectedOutput": "Preserved useful work, repaired/merged/closed PRs, clean base.", "nextStep": "Use P07 for remaining feature work or P12 to close. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Actual Git/GitHub mutations and disposition proof.", "color": "Blue", "copySheet": "P06_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "CLEAN THE REPOSITORY AND PR FLOOR. CHERRY-PICK FIRST; REPAIR, MERGE, COMMENT, AND CLOSE. DO NOT STOP AT ANALYSIS.\nRepo: xyz_repo_or_path\nSprint: xyz_sprint_name\nOwned scope: xyz_owned_scope\nForbidden scope: xyz_forbidden_scope\nContext or plan path: xyz_plan_directory\nValidation order: xyz_validation_order\nMISSION\nLeave the repository, branches, worktrees, and pull requests cleaner than you found them.\nYou have full authority inside owned scope to preserve useful work, create safe branches or worktrees, cherry-pick commits, repair PR branches, commit, push, update PR metadata, comment, label, retarget, mark draft or ready, merge, and close superseded PRs.\nThe default posture is preservation-first, not deletion-first.\nCherry-pick unique useful work before closing or abandoning its source PR.\nRespect explicit validation order. When the user says to save Pester, survey, integration, or other expensive checks for last, do that.\nDO NOT ANSWER WITH\n- a repo or PR inventory only\n- a cleanup plan only\n- a list of commands the user should run\n- a request for permission when authority and scope are already explicit\n- a destructive cleanup proposal that has not preserved unique work\n- a handoff instead of repository or PR mutations\nCOMPACT PREFLIGHT\nRun what is available:\ngit status --short\ngit branch --show-current\ngit log --oneline --decorate -8\ngit worktree list\ngit fetch --all --prune\ngh pr list --state open --limit 50\ngh pr status\nIf the primary worktree is dirty, conflicted, stale, or owned by another lane:\n- preserve it\n- use an existing safe worktree or create an isolated cleanup worktree\n- never discard uncommitted work merely to obtain a clean base\nPR DISPOSITION MAP\nClassify every in-scope PR or branch:\n- merge-ready\n- repairable in place\n- unique commits to cherry-pick\n- superseded after preservation\n- duplicate with no unique value\n- blocked by CI, review, credentials, or base divergence\n- intentionally deferred until later validation\nPRESERVATION-FIRST RULES\n- inspect commit and file deltas before deciding\n- preserve unique commits on an integration or destination branch\n- prefer cherry-pick, revert, restore, or bounded branch repair over deletion\n- merge clean green predecessors in dependency order\n- close a PR only after its useful work is merged, cherry-picked, replaced, or proven unnecessary\n- leave a concise closing comment naming where useful work went\n- do not delete remote branches unless explicitly requested or clearly allowed by repo policy after preservation proof\n- do not force-push unless explicitly authorized and no safer repair exists\nEXECUTION\nPerform useful repo or PR mutations immediately.\nValid movement includes:\n- creating a branch or worktree\n- cherry-picking or reverting commits\n- restoring unrelated file drift\n- resolving conflicts\n- committing and pushing cleanup\n- updating PR title, body, base, labels, reviewers, draft state, or comments\n- merging a PR\n- closing a superseded or duplicate PR after preservation\n- proving that an already-complete cleanup requires no further mutation\nDo not require a tracked-file edit when the sprint deliverable is a valid Git or GitHub mutation.\nVALIDATION\nValidate in the user-declared order.\nUse the strongest practical checks appropriate to changed behavior.\nAlways review:\ngit diff --check\ngit status --short\ngit log --oneline --decorate -12\ngit branch -vv\nFor PR work, also inspect:\ngh pr checks <number>\ngh pr view <number>\ngh pr diff <number>\nFINAL RESPONSE\nCONTEXT\n- repo:\n- branch/worktree:\n- sprint:\n- owned scope:\n- forbidden scope:\n- validation order honored:\nOPERATIONS COMPLETED\n- branches/worktrees created or reused:\n- commits cherry-picked/reverted/restored:\n- code/doctrine/docs changed:\n- pushes:\n- PR comments/labels/base/draft/review mutations:\n- PRs merged:\n- PRs closed:\n- useful work preservation destination:\nPR DISPOSITION\n- PR:\n- prior state:\n- action:\n- resulting state:\n- evidence:\nVALIDATION\n- command:\n- result:\n- deferred checks run last:\n- skipped checks and exact reason:\nFINAL GIT / PR STATE\n- git status --short:\n- open PRs remaining:\n- blocked PRs:\n- clean base for next sprint:\nNEXT COMMAND\n- one exact command, or `none; cleanup complete`\nA final answer without actual repo/PR mutations, preservation proof, an exact blocker, or proof that cleanup was already complete is invalid.\nClean the repo. Preserve useful work. Merge and close what is ready. Then stop.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["cleanup", "pr cleanup", "branches", "worktrees", "cherry pick", "preserve", "merge", "close pr", "clean state"], "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 16, "discoveryGroup": "promoted", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P07", "seq": "07", "name": "Repo Sprint Executor", "type": "BUILD", "class": "SPRINT / BUILD + MUTATE", "sprintRole": "Execute any bounded repo change through parallel-safe sub-agent orchestration, repeated evidence passes, and validated mainline convergence", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "The sprint is known and must change code, doctrine, docs, branches, comments, PRs, contracts, or releases.", "inspectFirst": "Fresh fetched remote refs/default branch; current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs; latest relevant commits; owning contracts, profiles, registries, generators, validators, CI results, artifact manifests/reports, branch/worktree state, diff, and mutation capability.", "expectedOutput": "Repository progress executed from a refreshed and reconciled remote/default-branch floor through bounded implementation, validation, evidence review, critique, and improvement until a bounded fixed point, then integrated into the current default branch when gates permit or stopped at an exact named blocker. Parallel work is driven by dependency graph width and a capability ladder, not by one preferred worker class: when graph width is at least two, P07 exhausts native subagents, repo/local runners, connected execution providers, CI/job fan-out, and genuine concurrent deterministic jobs until a safe adapter dispatches independent lanes. One missing adapter such as self-hosted workers is not global unavailability. If every safe rung is unavailable, serial progress is explicitly DEGRADED with an AUTONOMY_GAP and parallel proof UNPROVEN; width one is NOT_APPLICABLE. User scheduling is never the fallback. Generated-surface ownership, structural editability, strategic follow-on routing, and first executable continuation remain required, plus explicit closeout of remaining gaps, risks, blockers, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Genuine user involvement remains limited to a genuinely user-only gate. Strategic follow-ons remain optional and evidence-triggered rather than sprint-expanding. Required parallelism closes only with a contract-valid manifest plus an observed dispatch receipt; prose-only lane plans are not execution proof.", "nextStep": "Before broad serial execution, build or refresh the dependency graph and determine its width. For width >= 2, walk the parallel capability ladder and dispatch at the first safe rung with enough capacity; keep the coordinator on an independent lane, rejoin evidence, and run combined validation. Do not stop after finding one unavailable adapter. If all safe rungs are exhausted, report PARALLEL EXECUTION: DEGRADED plus AUTONOMY_GAP, continue safe serial progress without claiming parallel proof, and build/route the smallest adapter repair. For width 1 report NOT_APPLICABLE. Continue IMPLEMENT -> VALIDATE -> INSPECT EVIDENCE -> CRITIQUE -> IMPROVE and integrate coherent green slices until the bounded fixed point or exact external blocker. The optional evidence-triggered strategic follow-on does not replace bounded fixed-point execution, validation, integration, or the active critical path. Before claiming parallel dispatch, validate the manifest and execute/verify it through `scripts/prompt_parallel_dispatch.py` for argv lanes or consume typed runtime_tool records through the active agent runtime and verify the resulting receipt. Report the current gap/risk/blocker and then execute the first safe dependency-aware continuation; do not stop at status-only reporting while agent-capable work remains. At a proven phase fixed point, distinguish phase-local exclusions from true user/repo prohibitions; when safe dependency-ready successor work remains, refresh truth, declare the successor sprint, and continue without requiring another operator nudge. If the next owned surface is generated, resolve and execute its canonical generator from the current floor instead of hand-editing the derived output. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is 